TI  - Natural vegetation in the desert plains grassland
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - As an introduction to the discussion of plant associations, a brief description is given of the soils and climate of the area. The effective rainfall rather than total amount is an important factor in determining the features of a plant association. Two important major vegetative types occur in the area: (1) the black grama-tobosa and (2) curly mesquite-blue grama. The Desert Plains Grassland is thus considered as a true short grass association. Primary successions leading to establishment of the climax grasses are listed. 3 lines of evidence show that severe disturbance of the natural grass cover by heavy grazing, fire, cultivation, and erosion has resulted in decrease of the climax grasses and brought about predominance of desert-like shrubby vegetation in much of the area.
