AU  - Ferreira-Rodríguez, N
AU  - Iglesiasi, J
AU  - Pardo, I
TI  - Corbicula fluminea affecting supporting ecosystem services through nutrient and biogenic matter incorporation in invaded estuaries
T2  - FUNDAMENTAL AND APPLIED LIMNOLOGY
AB  - Non-native species have led to the degradation of many ecosystem services, while having the potential to enhance others. The present study analyzes long-term changes in nutrient composition of the water column after Corbicula fluminea introduction, following a regression discontinuity design. In addition, cross-sectional nutrient concentration and sediment composition data from uninvaded and invaded estuaries were compared by using a three-level univariate regression model. Long-term data showed that concentrations of ammonium and phosphate exceeded background nutrient concentrations in invaded estuaries after C.fluminea introduction. Cross-sectional data showed greater amounts of ammonium and phosphate in the water column of invaded estuaries. Sediments of invaded estuaries had higher organic matter and calcium carbonate concentrations. Altogether, present results suggest that C. fluminea could favor primary productivity via nutrient enrichment. Observed differences in the sediment composition of invaded estuaries may also alter ecosystem functioning. In one side, organic matter enrichment may favor specific macroinvertebrate functional groups. In another, increasing calcium concentrations in the water column by calcium carbonate dissolution may influence a positive feedback in the invasive potential of non-native bivalves. Overall, this study provides an example of how invasive species can influence supporting ecosystem services that should be taken into account when managing already invaded environments.
