AU  - Asamoah, J
ED  - Eliasson, B
ED  - Riemer, P
ED  - Wokaun, A
TI  - The uptake of energy conscious housing design in South Africa as a mitigation of emission of greenhouse gases
T2  - GREENHOUSE GAS CONTROL TECHNOLOGIES
CP  - 4th International Conference on Greenhouse Gas Control Technologies (GHGT-4)
AB  - Housing backlog in Africa is a real problem confronting many governments on the continent. Attempts to redress this backlog could bring other benefits on the global front. If one regards a house as a huge energy appliance, then the impetus to explore the big savings in avoided greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions is significant. This can be realised by simply adopting energy conscious design and demand-side management. Benefits to be gained from the implementation of this seemingly simple technology includes reduction in the use of fossil fuels, cost savings, improvement in health, improved levels of comfort and optimum use of natural resources.
   In existing houses, retrofits are being introduced to use energy optimally. Models and real life situations are now being showcased to emphasise the fact that energy efficient houses are not necessarily more expensive than conventional ones, but can result in win-win scenarios. Why the apparent dearth of such homes in Africa? There seems to be lack of capacity, education and awareness to the uptake of the above technology in the design of houses. In South Africa, there is a huge opportunity to avoid emission of GHG in the massive on-going Reconstruction and Development Programme's (RDP) housing scheme; through the adoption of energy conscious design. Some of the technologies currently available are compact fluorescent lights (CFL), photovoltaics (PV), sunstoves and proper orientation of houses to take advantage of solar energy. The perception that some of these technologies require huge initial capital outlays may hold in some cases. However governments and building societies could assist by promoting these technologies through buy downs and other financially friendly schemes. At the design stage, houses could be properly orientated to take advantage of daylight. Africa is endowed with abundant sunshine throughout the year. The above measures would reduce the demand for fossil fuels that pollute the atmosphere and also contribute to global climate change. Forest degradation arising out of uncontrolled cutting of trees for woodfuel and erosion from over grazing of grassland is rife in South Africa. This paper will highlight some of the technologies and demand side management; that are used to reduce the supply and demand for conventional forms of energy in the household.
