TI  - Eco-compensation for Grassland Restoration: a Case Study of the Xilingol Pasture in Inner Mongolia, China
T2  - ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE AND ENGINEERING
CP  - SREE Conference on Environmental Science and Engineering, CESE 2010
AB  - This paper provides an insight into eco-compensation instruments for grassland eco-systems in Inner Mongolia, Northern China. A key challenge for the eco-compensation mechanism is to sustain the economic viability of the local residents, who earn a large proportion of their income from traditional pasture-based production systems. Analysis of survey data from 240 households-showed that the ecological protection policies for grassland restoration are one of the major driving forces leading to a change in the regional income structure. The proportion of income from pasture-based activities had gradually decreased, while income from odd jobs and government subsidies had increased. The production concept and the management of the converted land had also changed. It was found that these changes contribute to the restoration of grassland ecosystems. However, the existing compensation level of the ecological restoration policies was too low to maintain the sustainable livelihood of the farmers in the region, particularly of specialized grazing-livestock farms. We analyzed the farmers income level and development capability to identify possible development strategies for the future. The results show that the households' income is relatively low compared to other regions in China. Due to lacking capital, many farmers have only a low development potential. A possible option to overcome this situation could be to organize the individual households in joint-groups in order to gather sufficient capital for further development. Possible investment strategies for ensuring long-term sustainable livelihood could include vegetable cultivation, co-management of grasslands and livestock, the development of the dairy sector and beef-cattle breeding.
